"(PDF) Spectral Similarity Measure Using Frequency Spectrum for Hyperspectral Image Classification (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/264743770_Spectral_Similarity_Measure_Using_Frequency_Spectrum_for_Hyperspectral_Image_Classification)\nciteturn165view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/264743770_Spectral_Similarity_Measure_Using_Frequency_Spectrum_for_Hyperspectral_Image_Classification\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1417\nL0: Article PDF Available\nL1: # Spectral Similarity Measure Using Frequency Spectrum for Hyperspectral Image Classification\nL2: \nL3:   * January 2015\nL4:   * cite0†IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters 12(1):130-134\nL5: \nL6: DOI:cite1†10.1109/LGRS.2014.2329183†doi.org L7: \nL8: Authors:\nL9: \nL10: cite2†Ke Wang L11: \nL12:   * cite3†Hohai University L13: \nL14: cite4†Bin Yong L15: \nL16: cite4†Bin Yong L17: \nL18:   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\nL19: \nL20: cite5†Xingfa Gu L21: \nL22:   * cite6†Guangzhou University L23: \nL24: cite7†Pengfeng Xiao L25: \nL26:   * cite8†Nanjing University L27: \nL28: Show all 5 authors Hide\nL29: cite9†Image†i1.rgstatic.net L30: \nL31: cite10†Download full-text PDF cite11†Read full-text L32: ## Abstract and Figures\nL33: A novel spectral similarity measure approach, which is named spectral frequency spectrum difference (SFSD), is proposed for hyperspectral image classification based on the frequency spectrum of spectral signature using the Fourier transform. Many important characteristics of spectral signature can be clearly reflected in the frequency spectrum. Therefore, the spectral similarity is defined as the frequency spectrum's difference between the target and reference signatures.\nL34: The frequency spectrum analysis in this study suggests that the magnitude values of the first few low-frequency components for spectral signature can effectively represent the spectral similarity. To balance the difference between the low- and high-frequency components, the frequency spectrum of the target spectral signature is taken as the normalized factor in the SFSD method. Next, the U.S.\nL35: Geological Survey spectral data and two hyperspectral remote sensing images were employed as test data in our validation experiments. The new SFSD proposed here was compared with the leading approaches in terms of the spectral discriminability and classification accuracy. Results show that the SFSD exhibits a relatively better performance and has more robust applications for hyperspectral image classification.\nL36: cite12†(First column) Original spectral signatures of smectite and xylan. (Second column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the first two harmonics . (Third column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the first ten harmonics. (Fourth column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the first 30 harmonics.…  L37: cite13†(First column) Original spectral signatures of water, anorthite, roscoelite, jarosite, and lawn grass. (Second column) Corresponding frequency spectra. (Third column) Frequency spectrum energy ratios (FS energy ratios) of the first nth harmonics to all harmonics. (Fourth column) Reconstruction by the first 46th harmonics.…  L38: \nL39: Figures - uploaded by cite7†Pengfeng Xiao L40: \nL41: Author content\nL42: \nL43: All figure content in this area was uploaded by Pengfeng Xiao\nL44: \nL45: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL46: cite14†Image: ResearchGate Logo L47: \nL48: Discover the world's research\nL49: \nL50:   * 25+ million members\nL51:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL52:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL53: cite15†Join for free L54: \nL55: Public Full-text 1\nL56: \nL57: Content uploaded by cite7†Pengfeng Xiao L58: \nL59: Author content\nL60: \nL61: All content in this area was uploaded by Pengfeng Xiao on Dec 22, 2014\nL62: \nL63: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL64: \nL65: 130 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 1, JANUARY 2015\nL66: \nL67: Spectral Similarity Measure Using Frequency\nL68: Spectrum for Hyperspectral Image Classiﬁcation\nL69: \nL70: Ke Wang, Bin Yong, Xingfa Gu, Pengfeng Xiao, Member, IEEE, and Xueliang Zhang\nL71: \nL72: Abstract—A novel spectral similarity measure approach, which\nL73: \nL74: is named spectral frequency spectrum difference (SFSD), is\nL75: \nL76: proposed for hyperspectral image classiﬁcation based on the\nL77: \nL78: frequency spectrum of spectral signature using the Fourier trans-\nL79: \nL80: form. Many important characteristics of spectral signature can\nL81: \nL82: be clearly reﬂected in the frequency spectrum. Therefore, the\nL83: spectral similarity is deﬁned as the frequency spectrum’s differ-\nL84: \nL85: ence between the target and reference signatures. The frequency\nL86: \nL87: spectrum analysis in this study suggests that the magnitude values\nL88: \nL89: of the ﬁrst few low-frequency components for spectral signature\nL90: \nL91: can effectively represent the spectral similarity. To balance the dif-\nL92: \nL93: ference between the low- and high-frequency components, the fre-\nL94: \nL95: quency spectrum of the target spectral signature is taken as the\nL96: normalized factor in the SFSD method. Next, the U.S. Geological\nL97: \nL98: Survey spectral data and two hyperspectral remote sensing images\nL99: \nL100: were employed as test data in our validation experiments. The new\nL101: \nL102: SFSD proposed here was compared with the leading approaches in\nL103: \nL104: terms of the spectral discriminability and classiﬁcation accuracy.\nL105: \nL106: Results show that the SFSD exhibits a relatively better perfor-\nL107: \nL108: mance and has more robust applications for hyperspectral image\nL109: \nL110: classiﬁcation.\nL111: Index Terms—Classiﬁcation, frequency spectrum, hyperspec-\nL112: \nL113: tral image, remote sensing, spectral similarity.\nL114: \nL115: I. I NTRODUCTION\nL116: \nL117: H YPERSPECTRAL sensors have hundreds of contiguous\nL118: \nL119: bands for spectral analysis, which provide more detailed\nL120: \nL121: spectral information of ground objects relative to multispectral\nL122: \nL123: sensors. Although the hyperspectral data exhibit great potential\nL124: \nL125: in the area of land cover classiﬁcation and object recognition,\nL126: \nL127: the Hughes phenomenon [1] still remains an open issue in\nL128: current hypersepctral image classiﬁcation. According to the\nL129: \nL130: principal theory of the Hughes phenomenon, as for a limited\nL131: \nL132: training set, the classiﬁcation accuracy tends to increase ﬁrst\nL133: \nL134: and then decrease, when the number of features increases.\nL135: \nL136: First, the widely used metric-based methods in hyperspec-\nL137: \nL138: tral image classiﬁcation can measure the spectral similarity\nL139: \nL140: Manuscript received February 9, 2014; revised April 5, 2014, April 25, 2014,\nL141: and June 1, 2014; accepted June 2, 2014. This work was supported in part by\nL142: \nL143: the National Key Basic Research Program under Grant 2011CB952001, by the\nL144: \nL145: International S&T Cooperation Program of China under Grant 2010DFA21880,\nL146: \nL147: by the National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant 51379056,\nL148: \nL149: and by the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2012M510053.\nL150: \nL151: K. Wang and B. Yong are with the State Key Laboratory of Hydrology-Water\nL152: Resources and Hydraulic Engineering, Hohai University, Nanjing 210098,\nL153: \nL154: China (e-mail: wangke_A@hotmail.com; yongbin_hhu@126.com).\nL155: \nL156: X. Gu is with the Institute of Remote Sensing and Digital Earth, Chinese\nL157: \nL158: Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100101, China (e-mail: xfgu@irsa.ac.cn).\nL159: \nL160: P. Xiao and X. Zhang are with the Department of Geographic Information\nL161: \nL162: Science, Nanjing University, Nanjing 210023, China (e-mail: xiaopf@nju.\nL163: \nL164: edu.cn; homelandzxl@gmail.com).\nL165: Color versions of one or more of the ﬁgures in this paper are available online\nL166: \nL167: at http://ieeexplore.ieee.org.\nL168: \nL169: Digital Object Identiﬁer 10.1109/LGRS.2014.2329183\nL170: \nL171: between target and reference spectral signatures. More impor-\nL172: \nL173: tantly, these methods are not inﬂuenced by the Hughes phe-\nL174: \nL175: nomenon. Among these methods, the Euclidean distance (ED)\nL176: \nL177: [2] is a common geometric-based vector-distance measurement\nL178: \nL179: that has been extended to N dimensions. However, the ED mea-\nL180: sure is insensitive to the shape and scaling of spectral signature.\nL181: \nL182: Hence, a normalized ED (NED) [3], which is divided by the\nL183: \nL184: mean of spectral signature, was developed. As well known,\nL185: \nL186: the NED is invariant to the multiplicative scaling. Second, the\nL187: \nL188: spectral angle mapping (SAM) proposed by Kruse et al. [4], [5]\nL189: \nL190: means the angle between two spectral signatures portrayed as\nL191: \nL192: vectors. Its advantage is that it is invariant with respect to the\nL193: \nL194: lengths of spectral signatures. Third, the spectral information\nL195: divergence (SID) developed by Chang [6] is used to retrieve\nL196: \nL197: spectral similarity using self-information. In addition, corre-\nL198: \nL199: lation is used to produce a spectral measure, including cross-\nL200: \nL201: correlation spectral measure [7] and spectral correlation mapper\nL202: \nL203: (SCM) [8], which is a derivative of Pearsonian correlation\nL204: \nL205: coefﬁcient and can eliminate negative correlation and minimize\nL206: \nL207: the shading effect. Finally, spectral gradient angle (SGA) [9]\nL208: \nL209: is invariant to scene geometry and illumination conditions and\nL210: takes into consideration slope changes within the vector in com-\nL211: \nL212: parison with other methods. Meanwhile, Du et al. [10] proposed\nL213: \nL214: two mixture measures, which are named StS/SsS, which con-\nL215: \nL216: siderably improved the spectral discriminability. Some research\nL217: \nL218: studies focus on the effectiveness of these measures for the\nL219: \nL220: application [11], [12]. These methods have been extensively\nL221: \nL222: used into discrimination of targets such as plant species [13]–\nL223: \nL224: [16] and material identiﬁcation [12], [17], [18].\nL225: This letter aims to develop a new spectral similarity mea-\nL226: \nL227: sure by using the frequency spectrum with Fourier transform.\nL228: \nL229: The differences between spectral signatures can result in the\nL230: \nL231: instinct discrepancy in the corresponding distribution of the\nL232: \nL233: frequency components’ magnitudes of these spectra. Thus, we\nL234: \nL235: proposed a metric-based method with frequency spectrum of\nL236: \nL237: spectral signature to address this issue. In previous spectral\nL238: \nL239: similarity measures, the whole spectral signature is involved\nL240: in the calculation. However, the frequency spectrum of spectral\nL241: \nL242: signature has the characteristic that the low-frequency spectrum\nL243: \nL244: contains most of the original signature’s energy and the high-\nL245: \nL246: frequency spectrum only contains some irrelative information.\nL247: \nL248: Thus, few low-frequency spectra can be used to reconstruct\nL249: \nL250: the original spectral signature. High frequencies are excluded\nL251: \nL252: to improve the performance of the measure. Furthermore, the\nL253: \nL254: computation cost can be reduced as expected because the\nL255: number of magnitudes involving into the new measure is much\nL256: \nL257: less than the length of spectral signature. Finally, we employed\nL258: \nL259: the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) spectral library and two\nL260: \nL261: hyperspectral images as experimental data to compare and\nL262: \nL263: validate the algorithmic efﬁciency for this new method.\nL264: \nL265: 1545-598X © 2014 IEEE. Personal use is permitted, but republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission.\nL266: See http://www.ieee.org/publications_standards/publications/rights/index.html for more information.\nL267: \nL268: WANG et al.: SPECTRAL SIMILARITY MEASURE USING FREQUENCY SPECTRUM FOR IMAGE CLASSIFICATION 131\nL269: \nL270: Fig. 1. (First column) Original spectral signatures of smectite and xylan.\nL271: \nL272: (Second column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the ﬁrst two har-\nL273: \nL274: monics. (Third column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the ﬁrst ten\nL275: \nL276: harmonics. (Fourth column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the ﬁrst\nL277: 30 harmonics.\nL278: \nL279: In the next section, we analyze the frequency spectrum of\nL280: \nL281: different ground objects’ spectra and propose a new spectral\nL282: \nL283: similarity measure in frequency domain. A presentation of the\nL284: \nL285: experimental results and analysis for the new algorithm follows\nL286: \nL287: in Section III. Summarizing remarks and conclusions ﬁnalize\nL288: \nL289: the letter in Section IV.\nL290: \nL291: II. M ETHODOLOGY\nL292: \nL293: A. Fourier Analysis of the Spectral Signature\nL294: \nL295: In 1822, Fourier pointed out that a real-valued periodic\nL296: period-p function can be expressed as the sum of sinusoidal\nL297: \nL298: oscillations of various frequencies, magnitudes, and phase\nL299: \nL300: shifts. Thus, a signal can be given by a complete description\nL301: \nL302: using sines or cosines. The process of calculating the Fourier\nL303: \nL304: coefﬁcient is deﬁned as Fourier transform, which is one of the\nL305: \nL306: most commonly used tools for signal processing. Let f(t)be a\nL307: \nL308: discrete signal; the Fourier transform of f(t)is deﬁned as\nL309: \nL310: F(u)=\nL311: \nL312: R\nL313: \nL314: f(t)e jut dt, j =√1(1)\nL315: where u is the frequency. We use frequency spectrum to analyze\nL316: \nL317: the spectral signature of each pixel in hyperspectral data. Be-\nL318: \nL319: cause the spectral signature is discrete, the corresponding fre-\nL320: \nL321: quency spectrum can be obtained by discrete Fourier transform\nL322: \nL323: (DFT), which is deﬁned by\nL324: \nL325: F(k)=\nL326: \nL327: N−1\nL328: \nL329: \nL330: \nL331: n=0\nL332: \nL333: f(n)e−j 2 π\nL334: \nL335: N nk (2)\nL336: \nL337: where N is the length of the spectral signature. Let f(x)be the\nL338: \nL339: original signal and F(u)be the Fourier transform of f(x).The\nL340: energy of the signal is equal to the frequency spectrum energy\nL341: \nL342: according to the Parseval theorem, which is deﬁned as\nL343: \nL344: N−1\nL345: \nL346: \nL347: \nL348: x=0 |f(x)|2=1\nL349: \nL350: N\nL351: \nL352: N−1\nL353: \nL354: \nL355: \nL356: u=0 |F(u)|2.(3)\nL357: \nL358: The spectral signatures (ENVI spectral library) of the smec-\nL359: \nL360: tite with 0.717–2.506 μ m wavelength range and 1790 bands\nL361: \nL362: and xylan with 0.4–2.5 μ m wavelength range and 826 bands\nL363: \nL364: are taken as an example for the frequency spectrum analysis\nL365: \nL366: (see Fig. 1). In order to show the contribution of low frequen-\nL367: cies, ﬁrst few harmonics are used to reconstruct the original\nL368: \nL369: signatures. As shown in the second column in Fig. 1, it can\nL370: \nL371: be intelligibly seen that the sum of the ﬁrst two harmonics\nL372: \nL373: is just a wave crest, where we cannot easily distinguish the\nL374: \nL375: smectite from xylan. In the third column in Fig. 1, there are\nL376: \nL377: Fig. 2. (First column) Original spectral signatures of water, anorthite,\nL378: \nL379: roscoelite, jarosite, and lawn grass. (Second column) Corresponding frequency\nL380: spectra. (Third column) Frequency spectrum energy ratios (FS energy ratios)\nL381: \nL382: of the ﬁrst n th harmonics to all harmonics. (Fourth column) Reconstruction by\nL383: \nL384: the ﬁrst 46th harmonics.\nL385: \nL386: still visible differences between original signatures and results\nL387: \nL388: reconstructed by the ﬁrst ten harmonics. Although there are\nL389: \nL390: still vital details missed in the fourth column in Fig. 1, the\nL391: \nL392: reconstruction from the ﬁrst 30 harmonics is much better than\nL393: \nL394: from the ﬁrst ten harmonics and is close to the original spectral\nL395: signature. Thus, some features with stronger change and detail\nL396: \nL397: information would appear with the frequency being higher.\nL398: \nL399: Conclusively, the representation of the original spectral\nL400: \nL401: signature’s outline can be satisﬁed by using the ﬁrst few har-\nL402: \nL403: monics, although more harmonics can retrieve better recon-\nL404: \nL405: structions. Thus, the ﬁrst few harmonics can be used to deﬁne\nL406: \nL407: the spectral similarity measure.\nL408: \nL409: B. SFSD\nL410: \nL411: Five typical USGS spectral signatures, including water, anor-\nL412: thite, roscoelite, jarosite, and lawn grass, are employed for fre-\nL413: \nL414: quency spectrum analysis. First, these signatures are resampled\nL415: \nL416: with the interval of 0.002 μ m and length (see the ﬁrst column\nL417: \nL418: in Fig. 2) of 1385. The frequency spectra of these signatures\nL419: \nL420: are shown in the second row in Fig. 2. Note that most of\nL421: \nL422: the frequency spectrum energy is concentrated around direct\nL423: \nL424: current (dc) component and the energy of high frequency is\nL425: \nL426: much less than that of low frequency.\nL427: As shown in Fig. 2, there are remarkable differences of\nL428: \nL429: the frequency spectrum characteristics from these ﬁve typical\nL430: \nL431: spectral signatures. For example, from the frequency spectrum\nL432: \nL433: of water’s spectral signature, its dc component is much lower\nL434: \nL435: than those of the other ground objects. Moreover, the distri-\nL436: \nL437: bution of the frequency spectrum is different among these ﬁve\nL438: \nL439: spectral signatures. Thus, the difference of magnitude spectrum\nL440: \nL441: (DMS) at the i th frequency components between the target and\nL442: reference spectral signatures is deﬁned as the spectral similarity\nL443: \nL444: measure, which is expressed by\nL445: \nL446: DMS =\nL447: \nL448: n\nL449: \nL450: \nL451: \nL452: i=1 |MoTS(i)−MoRS(i)|(4)\nL453: \nL454: 132 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 1, JANUARY 2015\nL455: \nL456: where i means the i th frequency component; n is the length\nL457: \nL458: of the spectral signature; and MoTS(i)and MoRS(i)are the\nL459: \nL460: magnitude of the i th frequency component for the target and\nL461: \nL462: reference spectral signatures, respectively.\nL463: \nL464: The frequency spectrum descends from the dc component\nL465: to the higher frequency component; thus, the DMS becomes\nL466: \nL467: smaller when parameter i increases. It may result in that\nL468: \nL469: the difference between target and reference spectra at higher\nL470: \nL471: frequency components is ignored. Thus, it is necessary to\nL472: \nL473: normalize the DMS to obtain a better measure. The spectral\nL474: \nL475: frequency spectrum difference (SFSD) can be deﬁned as\nL476: \nL477: SFSD =\nL478: \nL479: N\nL480: \nL481: \nL482: \nL483: i=1\nL484: \nL485: |MoTS(i)−MoRS(i)|\nL486: \nL487: MoTS(i)(5)\nL488: \nL489: where N is the number of frequency components involved in\nL490: \nL491: the measure.\nL492: Because the frequency spectrum is symmetric with respect\nL493: \nL494: to the dc component (see the second column in Fig. 2), N\nL495: \nL496: in (5) should be shorter than half the length of the spectral\nL497: \nL498: signature. Meanwhile, the components involved in the SFSD\nL499: \nL500: method should begin from the dc component to the high-order\nL501: \nL502: frequency component in order. Furthermore, the efﬁciency of\nL503: \nL504: the proposed measure is related to the parameter N, which\nL505: \nL506: should be kept to be as small as possible.\nL507: Considering the fact that the frequency spectrum energy ratio\nL508: \nL509: of the ﬁrst few harmonics decreases to some extent when the\nL510: \nL511: length of spectral signature increases, we employ the ﬁxed ratio\nL512: \nL513: of low-order harmonics to develop the new measure. According\nL514: \nL515: to the power spectrum of these spectral signatures, the ratios\nL516: \nL517: of the ﬁrst n th harmonics’ frequency spectrum energy to total\nL518: \nL519: energy are plotted in the third column in Fig. 2. The ratios\nL520: \nL521: for the ﬁrst 46th harmonics in the spectral signatures of water,\nL522: anorthite, roscoelite, jarosite, and lawn grass reach to 99.7%,\nL523: \nL524: 99.9%, 99.9%, 99.9%, and 99.9%, respectively.\nL525: \nL526: The fourth column in Fig. 2 illustrates the reconstruction\nL527: \nL528: from the ﬁrst 46th harmonics of these ﬁve original spectral\nL529: \nL530: signatures using inverse DFT. In addition, they achieve excel-\nL531: \nL532: lent ﬁtting of the original signatures. Thus, it is proven that\nL533: \nL534: the ﬁrst few low-order harmonics, namely, low frequencies, can\nL535: \nL536: precisely describe the original spectral signatures and that there\nL537: is no need to involve all frequencies’ magnitudes to calculate\nL538: \nL539: the SFSD.\nL540: \nL541: According to the preceding analysis, in our study, 99.7% of\nL542: \nL543: the frequency spectrum energy is taken to determine the number\nL544: \nL545: of low-order harmonics involving the SFSD. Because the ratio\nL546: \nL547: between 46 and the half of band number is only lower than\nL548: \nL549: 1/15, this value is used for separating the number of low-order\nL550: \nL551: harmonics, which is deﬁned by\nL552: \nL553: N=[α·n](6)\nL554: \nL555: where n is the half band number of spectral signature, the ratio\nL556: α is set as 1/15, and [] is the rounding operation.\nL557: \nL558: III. E XPERIMENTS AND R ESULTS\nL559: \nL560: A. Experiment I: Spectra in USGS Spectral Library\nL561: \nL562: USGS data shown in Fig. 2, with spectral coverage from\nL563: \nL564: 0.2 to 3.0 μ m, are still taken as experimental data. We aim\nL565: \nL566: TAB LE I\nL567: \nL568: RSDE SOF SFSD, SAM, SID, SCM, SGA NED,\nL569: \nL570: ED, AND S T S/S S S IN USGS D ATA\nL571: \nL572: to evaluate the spectral discriminability of the proposed mea-\nL573: \nL574: sure by comparing the previous method, including SAM, SID,\nL575: SCM, SGA, NED, ED, and StS/SsS, based on relative spectral\nL576: \nL577: discriminatory entropy (RSDE) proposed by Chang [6]. The\nL578: \nL579: calculation results of RSDEs are shown in Table I.\nL580: \nL581: In terms of the discrimination for water, we can clearly see\nL582: \nL583: that RSDEs for all methods are close, except for SID and\nL584: \nL585: StS/SsS. Although RSDEs of SFSD and SGA are higher than\nL586: \nL587: the other methods, the brightness of the water being much lower\nL588: \nL589: than the other four spectral signatures results in that the spectral\nL590: difference is distinct between the spectral signature of the water\nL591: \nL592: and the other four spectra. Thus, water can be easily identiﬁed\nL593: \nL594: using these methods, including SFSD.\nL595: \nL596: As to the anorthite, roscoelite, jarosite, and lawn grass, the\nL597: \nL598: RSDE of the proposed method is about 0.2, which is much\nL599: \nL600: lower than the other methods (over 0.3). Particularly, all RSDEs\nL601: \nL602: of SAM, SID, SCM, SGA, NED, and ED are over 0.4. The\nL603: \nL604: RSDEs of StS/SsS range from 0.3 to 0.55, but they are still\nL605: higher than SFSD. Thus, it is more effective than the others in\nL606: \nL607: the discrimination of these four ground objects.\nL608: \nL609: B. Experiment II: AVIRIS Image in Salinas Valley\nL610: \nL611: The second experimental hyperspectral data, which is an\nL612: \nL613: Airborne Visible/Infrared Imaging Spectrometer (AVIRIS) im-\nL614: \nL615: age, were acquired over Salinas Valley, CA, in 1998. The full\nL616: \nL617: image contains 217 ×512 pixels and 220 bands with spectral\nL618: \nL619: coverage ranging from 0.4 to 2.45 μ m. In addition, it was\nL620: taken at a low altitude with the spatial resolution of 3.7 m.\nL621: \nL622: A subscene of these data comprising 83 ×86 pixels is used\nL623: \nL624: for classiﬁcation experiment. The data set shown in Fig. 3(a)\nL625: \nL626: presents a vegetation scenario, including broccoli, corn, and\nL627: \nL628: lettuce romaine with different growth stages. In addition, the\nL629: \nL630: classes of broccoli and corn senescent are mixed with some\nL631: \nL632: green weeds. The main advantage of this data set is that there\nL633: \nL634: is a ground truth map shown in Fig. 3(b), including six land\nL635: cover classes prepared at the time of image acquisition. Fig. 3(c)\nL636: \nL637: shows the average of each class’s spectral signature from all\nL638: \nL639: labeled pixels in the ground truth data and is taken as the\nL640: \nL641: reference spectral signature. The pixel is classiﬁed to the class i,\nL642: \nL643: if the spectral similarity measure between the target pixel’s and\nL644: \nL645: reference spectral signatures of class i is smaller than the other\nL646: \nL647: classes. The difference of spectral signatures between broccoli\nL648: green weeds and lettuce romaine 7wk is distinct, whereas the\nL649: \nL650: other signatures are similar, particularly lettuce romaine 4wk,\nL651: \nL652: lettuce romaine 6wk, and corn senesced green weeds.\nL653: \nL654: The classiﬁcation results of this subscence by using SFSD,\nL655: \nL656: SAM, SID, SCM, SGA, NED, ED, and StS/SsS are illustrated\nL657: \nL658: in Fig. 4. Visually, all these eight methods have good perfor-\nL659: \nL660: mance on classiﬁcation results. The lettuce romaine 6wk is\nL661: WANG et al.: SPECTRAL SIMILARITY MEASURE USING FREQUENCY SPECTRUM FOR IMAGE CLASSIFICATION 133\nL662: \nL663: Fig. 3. (a) False color hyperspectral image (bands 18, 30, and 68).\nL664: \nL665: (b) Ground truth of the labeled area with six classes. C1=Broccoli green weeds\nL666: \nL667: (broccoli mixed with some green weeds); C2=Corn senesced green weeds\nL668: \nL669: (corn senescent mixed with some greeen weeds); C3=Lettuce romaine 4wk;\nL670: \nL671: C4=Lettuce romaine 5wk; C5=Lettuce romaine 6wk; C6=Lettuce romaine\nL672: 7wk. (c) Average of each class’s spectral signature from all labeled pixels in the\nL673: \nL674: ground truth data. Note that “wk” means “weeks”.\nL675: \nL676: Fig. 4. Classiﬁcation results using the (a) SFSD, (b) SAM, (c) SID, (d) SCM,\nL677: \nL678: (e) SGA, (f) NED, (g) ED, and (h) STS/SSS methods.\nL679: \nL680: TAB LE I I\nL681: \nL682: C LASSIFICATION A CCURACIES OF THE SFSD, SAM, SID, SCM, SGA,\nL683: \nL684: NED, ED, AND S T S/S S SM ETHODS O VER S ALINAS V ALLEY.T HE\nL685: \nL686: A CCURACY M EASURES I NCLUDE PA, UA, OA, AA,\nL687: \nL688: AND K APPA C OEFFIC IENT\nL689: magniﬁcently classiﬁed by all these methods. As to the class of\nL690: \nL691: broccoli green weeds, good classiﬁcation performance can be\nL692: \nL693: obtained by SFSD, SAM, SCM, SGA, NED, and ED. However,\nL694: \nL695: some pixels belonging to broccoli green weeds are classiﬁed as\nL696: \nL697: lettuce romaine 7wk using SID and StS/SsS. Note that the worst\nL698: \nL699: result is the corn senesced green weeds, which are misclassiﬁed\nL700: \nL701: as other classes.\nL702: \nL703: In order to analyze the effectiveness of the proposed method,\nL704: producer’s accuracy (PA), user’s accuracy (UA), overall accu-\nL705: \nL706: racy (OA), average accuracy (AA), and Kappa coefﬁcient are\nL707: \nL708: employed to evaluate the classiﬁcation result. The accuracies\nL709: \nL710: of these eight methods are shown in Table II.\nL711: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(